```tex
\chapter{Once Upon a Time}

In a quiet corner of the park, where the sun filtered through ancient oak trees,
two ducks sat by the edge of a pond that shimmered like a mirror of forgotten dreams.
The first duck, whom we shall call A, traced patterns in the water with its beak.
The second duck, B, watched the clouds drift across the sky in slow procession.

\section{The Pond}
The pond was not an ordinary pond. It reflected not the sky above
but the sky of another world --- a world where the laws of mathematics
were written in the ripples of water and the curves of duck feathers.

Duck A spoke first: \textit{``The water remembers everything.''}
Duck B tilted its head: \textit{``Then why does it keep changing?''}

\section{The Geometry of Duckness}
To be a duck is to exist at the intersection of two planes:
the plane of the water and the plane of the sky. A duck is a living
proof that surfaces can be bridged, that above and below are merely
perspectives on the same continuum.

Duck A said: \textit{``We are shadows on the water.''}
Duck B replied: \textit{``No -- the water is a shadow of us.''}

\section{The Forever Ripple}
A leaf fell from the oak tree above, landing on the pond with a soft touch.
The ripples spread outward in perfect circles, each one containing
the memory of the one before it. The ducks watched in silence.

\textit{``That,'' said Duck A, ``is a story.''}
\textit{``Every story is a ripple,'' said Duck B.}
\textit{``It begins somewhere, spreads everywhere, and never truly ends.''}

\section{Coda}
The sun began to set, painting the pond in shades of amber and violet.
The two ducks remained by the water, watching the ripples carry
the light into the infinite. And the park, which had always been there,
became a little more real than it had been before.

Once upon a time, there were two ducks in a park.
They are still there.
They will always be there.
```

